## Developer / reviewer agents

Implementation work goes through the `paper-to-code` plugin's two subagents:
- `paper-to-code:developer`: writes tests first on synthetic data, implements in the package, presents the results in notebooks,
  runs ruff/pytest/nbmake, and hands back a structured report. Never commits.
- `paper-to-code:reviewer`: reviews the uncommitted diff against the paper and returns `VERDICT: APPROVE | CHANGES_REQUESTED`
  with evidence-backed findings. It is read-only: it has no Edit/Write tools, and the plugin's PreToolUse hook blocks any of its
  Bash commands that are not on a read-only allowlist.
- `/paper-to-code:dev-review <task>` is the user-invoked orchestrator. The main session alternates developer → reviewer for up to
  3 rounds, resuming the same developer and starting a fresh reviewer each round. It leaves changes uncommitted, and requires at least
  one commit and a clean working tree at the start, so the reviewer only sees the task's changes.
